    title: "Testing",
    summary: "Write tests beside application modules and rerun only the tests related to a change."
  }

  %DocsSection{ title: "Colocated Tests" }
    :markdown
      Test files live anywhere under `src` and end in `.test.rb`. They are test entrypoints, not application dependencies. A test can import the component or module beside it:

      ```ruby
      Button = import("./Button.haml")

      def test_renders_button_children
        screen = render(Button, "Save", type: "submit")
        button = screen.get_by_role(:button, name: "Save")

        assert_equal("submit", button["type"])
      end
      ```

      The example adapter turns each test module into a Minitest test case. Top-level `test_*`, `setup`, `teardown`, and helper methods become instance methods on that case. Assertions come from Minitest, so test files do not need a class or a Minitest require.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Run And Watch" }
    :markdown
      Run the tests and keep watching:

      ```sh
      bin/test
      ```

      Use `bin/test --run` for one run. `CI` also selects one-run mode automatically. Each batch runs in a fresh worker process, so test state cannot leak into later batches or the development server.

      Klenod collects every test's eager and lazy dependency closure. Editing a test reruns that test. Editing an imported module or any transitive dependency reruns every related test. Added tests run immediately, removed tests leave the suite, and unrelated changes do not trigger a test run.

      The development server ignores `.test.rb` changes. The separate test watcher handles them, so editing a test reruns it without producing an unrelated server update.

  %DocsSection{ title: "Framework Helpers" }
    :markdown
      Klenod does not choose a test library or rendering API. The example framework renders the component to HTML, parses that HTML with Nokolexbor, and returns a queryable fragment:

      ```ruby
      screen = render(Button, "Read the docs", href: "/docs/assets")
      link = screen.get_by_role(:link, name: "Read the docs")

      assert_equal("/docs/assets", link["href"])
      ```

      `screen.html` returns the rendered string and `screen.fragment` returns the Nokolexbor document fragment. `within(node)` creates a query scope for that node's descendants.

      Role, text, and CSS queries provide `get_by_*`, `query_by_*`, `get_all_by_*`, and `query_all_by_*` variants. A singular query raises when it finds multiple nodes. `get` queries also raise when nothing matches, while `query` variants return `nil` or an empty array. Errors show the query, candidates, and rendered HTML. `has_role?`, `has_text?`, and `has_css?` provide small matcher-style predicates.

      Prefer roles and accessible names. The role implementation models useful static HTML semantics, including labels and ARIA naming, but it is not a complete browser accessibility tree. It cannot test layout, computed styles, focus, events, or JavaScript behavior. The helpers intentionally provide no fake click or focus API; use browser tests for those behaviors.

      Text queries normalize whitespace and return the most specific matching elements rather than every matching ancestor. CSS queries are available as an escape hatch. Test rendering also keeps authored class names and omits generated classes for scoped tag selectors, but class selectors should not be the default way to describe user-visible behavior.

